# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  theourgia_term.la — the terminal model of Theourgia Stage 10: output
#  bytes to lines, the line editor and its history, soft wrap, the keymap.
#
#  WHAT. A terminal is a value (a record, below). TERM_KIT builds the
#  operations on it (WM_DESIGN.md, "theourgia_term.la"):
#
#    new(prompt)                an empty terminal
#    write(t)(bytes)            feed program output (rules below)
#    flush(t)                   end the partial line if it is non-empty
#    key(t)(ch)                 append ch to the input line
#    back(t)                    drop the input line's last character
#    kill_line(t)               empty the input line
#    submit(t)                  la k. k(line)(t'): see below
#    hist_prev(t), hist_next(t) walk history into the input line
#    clear(t)                   drop all output
#    set_prompt(t)(p)           change the prompt
#    rows(t)(cols)(n)(idle)     the last n display rows, soft-wrapped
#    keychar(code)(shift)       evdev key code -> US-layout character
#
#  WHY. The window manager draws each window from rows(...) every frame and
#  feeds write(...) every byte a running command prints, so these two are the
#  hot paths; the rest run once per key press.
#
#  HOW IT IS USED. Build the kit ONCE at startup and destructure it once:
#
#    TERM_KIT(str_at)(ord)(str_to_int)(str_len)(chr)(concat)(str_eq)
#            (add)(sub)(mul)(div)(mod)(lt)(int_eq)
#      (la new. la write. la flush. la key. la back. la kill_line. la submit.
#       la hist_prev. la hist_next. la clear. la set_prompt. la rows. la keychar.
#         ...)
#
#  The record's fields come IN THAT ORDER: new write flush key back kill_line
#  submit hist_prev hist_next clear set_prompt rows keychar. Booleans are
#  Church (TRUE = la t. la f. t): rows' idle and keychar's shift take one.
#  Lists are Scott (NIL = la n. la c. n(n), CONS(h)(t) = la n. la c. c(h)(t)):
#  rows returns one.
#
#  OUTPUT (write). Bytes are taken one at a time:
#    32..126          appended to the partial line
#    10 (\n)          ends the partial line (it becomes a completed line)
#    13 (\r)          ignored
#    9 (\t)           spaces up to the next multiple of 8 (always at least one)
#    8 (\b)           drops the partial line's last character, if any
#    27 [ ... F       CSI: skipped up to and including the final byte F,
#                     0x40..0x7E
#    27 X             any other escape: ESC and the one byte X skipped
#    >= 0xC0          a UTF-8 lead byte: shown as one '?'
#    0x80..0xBF       UTF-8 continuation bytes: dropped
#    other controls and 127: ignored
#  The escape state survives between calls, so a chunk may end in the middle
#  of a sequence (a lone ESC, or a CSI cut in two).
#
#  SUBMIT. submit(t) first ends a non-empty partial output line (so the echo
#  starts a line of its own), then appends prompt + input as an output line,
#  empties the input, records the input in history (an empty input is not
#  recorded) and stops any history walk. It returns la k. k(input)(t').
#
#  HISTORY. hist_prev replaces the input with the next older entry (no change
#  at the oldest); hist_next with the next newer one, and stepping past the
#  newest restores the line that was being typed when the walk began (no
#  change when not walking). Editing a recalled line edits the input only; the
#  history entry is not changed.
#
#  CLEAR drops the completed lines and the partial line; input, prompt,
#  history and the escape state are kept.
#
#  ROWS. The logical lines are the completed lines (oldest first) and then a
#  last line: prompt + input + the cursor chr(127) when idle is TRUE, the
#  partial output line otherwise (even when it is empty). A line of length
#  L > 0 takes ceil(L/cols) rows, cut every cols bytes; an empty line takes
#  one row. rows returns the last n rows, top to bottom, exactly n strings,
#  with "" rows at the top when there are fewer. Only the lines those n rows
#  come from are visited, so the cost is O(n) rows, not O(history). cols < 1
#  is taken as 1; n < 1 gives NIL.
#
#  KEYCHAR. The US layout: KEY_1..KEY_0, MINUS, EQUAL, Q..P, LEFTBRACE,
#  RIGHTBRACE, A..L, SEMICOLON, APOSTROPHE, GRAVE, BACKSLASH, Z..M, COMMA, DOT,
#  SLASH and SPACE type their character, shift giving the shifted symbol or
#  the capital. Every other code — ESC, BACKSPACE, TAB, ENTER, the modifiers,
#  arrows, keypad, function keys — gives "". The KEY_* glyphs exported below
#  are the codes the window manager binds; they are plain constants, read once
#  at startup.
#
#  RECORD. A terminal is la k. k(R)(E)(I)(Pr)(H)(J)(V), and R, the output,
#  is la k. k(L)(N)(S)(C)(K):
#    L  completed lines, newest first        N  how many are stored
#    S  the partial line's full 64-byte segments, newest first
#    C  the partial line's tail (fewer than 64 bytes)
#    K  the partial line's length (= 64·|S| + |C|)
#    E  escape state: 0 none, 1 after ESC, 2 inside CSI
#    I  input line   Pr  prompt   H  history, newest first
#    J  history walk: 0 not walking, j >= 1 showing entry j-1 of H
#    V  the line saved when the walk began
#  Callers treat it as opaque. At most 500 completed lines are kept. To keep
#  the cap O(1) per line, the list is trimmed to the newest 500 only when it
#  reaches 1000; rows never looks past the newest 500, so the observable
#  history is exactly the last 500 lines. The partial line is kept in 64-byte
#  segments so a long line without a newline costs O(n log n), not O(n^2).
#
#  PERFORMANCE. Kit style throughout (WM_DESIGN.md): the builtins are kit
#  parameters, the constants are computed once when the kit is built, every
#  helper is let-bound inside the kit, loops recurse by self-application
#  (g(g)) rather than Z, and booleans are applied directly to thunks. Nothing
#  names a glyph or a builtin after startup (checked with the gdb trap). The
#  costs that remain were measured on the VM and shaped the code:
#    - a VM operation costs ~25 ns, so the per-byte loop for printable runs
#      (RUNP) is a two-parameter loop doing the least work per byte: one
#      bounds-and-class test (a 256-entry class string) and one append;
#    - CLOSE rescans a lambda's body (~2-3 ns a byte) once per curried
#      parameter, so write's per-byte steps take (o)(i)(R) only, with the
#      per-call values (the bytes, their length, the exit) applied once per
#      write call, and control bytes are pure R -> R' steps chosen as VALUES
#      (no thunks); a run that ends at '\n' ends the line directly;
#    - a name lookup walks the environment newest binding first (~2.4 ns a
#      cell), so each group of hot code is preceded by a block re-binding
#      the names it uses, least used first;
#    - '\b' would cost a slice of up to 63 bytes each if taken one at a
#      time (there is no substring builtin), so a run of '\b' is counted and
#      dropped at once (DROP: whole segments popped, one slice), and a '\b'
#      right after a printable run cancels the run's last byte as the run
#      ends (RBS), slicing nothing.
#  On the VM: mixed output (text, tabs, colour CSIs, UTF-8) ~1.6-1.9 us a
#  byte, printable runs ~1.4, a bare control byte ~5, the start of a
#  printable run ~7; a '\b' in a run of them ~0.8; overstrike (_\bA) ~5 a
#  byte, the cost of its two-byte runs; rows(80)(40) over 500 stored short
#  lines ~0.1 ms, 40 wrapped rows ~2 ms (slicing a row costs per byte).
#  gate_wm_term.sh measures these against the cost of one glyph-table walk
#  in the same program (~10 us).
#
#  OUT OF SCOPE, honestly: cursor movement and the rest of VT100 (CSI is
#  skipped, not obeyed), colour, a cursor inside the input line (editing is at
#  its end only), Caps Lock and non-US layouts, wide characters (a UTF-8
#  character is one '?'), and history size limits (history grows by one entry
#  per non-empty submitted line).
#
#  Pure Lingua Adamica: the same results on the C host and the native VM.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

export TERM_KIT KEY_ESC KEY_BACKSPACE KEY_TAB KEY_ENTER KEY_UP KEY_DOWN KEY_LEFT KEY_RIGHT KEY_LEFTSHIFT KEY_RIGHTSHIFT KEY_LEFTCTRL KEY_RIGHTCTRL KEY_LEFTALT KEY_RIGHTALT KEY_LEFTMETA KEY_RIGHTMETA KEY_Q KEY_T KEY_C KEY_E KEY_H KEY_J KEY_K KEY_L KEY_MINUS KEY_EQUAL

# ── Key codes (linux/input-event-codes.h) ──
glyph KEY_ESC        = 1
glyph KEY_MINUS      = 12
glyph KEY_EQUAL      = 13
glyph KEY_BACKSPACE  = 14
glyph KEY_TAB        = 15
glyph KEY_Q          = 16
glyph KEY_E          = 18
glyph KEY_T          = 20
glyph KEY_ENTER      = 28
glyph KEY_LEFTCTRL   = 29
glyph KEY_H          = 35
glyph KEY_J          = 36
glyph KEY_K          = 37
glyph KEY_L          = 38
glyph KEY_LEFTSHIFT  = 42
glyph KEY_C          = 46
glyph KEY_RIGHTSHIFT = 54
glyph KEY_LEFTALT    = 56
glyph KEY_RIGHTCTRL  = 97
glyph KEY_RIGHTALT   = 100
glyph KEY_UP         = 103
glyph KEY_LEFT       = 105
glyph KEY_RIGHT      = 106
glyph KEY_DOWN       = 108
glyph KEY_LEFTMETA   = 125
glyph KEY_RIGHTMETA  = 126

# ── The kit ──
# LET(v)(la name. rest) binds name to v for rest; it is itself bound once, as
# the kit's first act, so not even LET is looked up in the glyph table.
glyph TERM_KIT = la str_at. la ord. la str_to_int. la str_len. la chr. la concat. la str_eq.
    la add. la sub. la mul. la div. la mod. la lt. la int_eq.
 (la LET.
  # constants, computed once
  LET(0)(la K0. LET(1)(la K1. LET(2)(la K2. LET(3)(la K3. LET(4)(la K4. LET(5)(la K5.
  LET(6)(la K6. LET(7)(la K7. LET(8)(la K8. LET(9)(la K9. LET(10)(la K10. LET(27)(la K27.
  LET(64)(la K64. LET(91)(la K91. LET(127)(la K127. LET(192)(la K192.
  LET(500)(la CAP. LET(1000)(la CAP2.
  LET(K0)(la D.
  LET(la t. la f. f)(la FF.
  # The per-byte code's builtins get one-letter names (short bodies to rescan).
  # A name is found by walking the environment newest binding first, so each
  # group of hot code below is preceded by a block that RE-BINDS the names it
  # uses, least used first: every lookup then ends within a few cells. The
  # blocks run once, when the kit is built.
  LET(str_at)(la A. LET(str_eq)(la W.
  # CLS: byte b's class, 'p' for printable (32..126), 'n' for the rest
  LET("nnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnpppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnn")(la CLS.
  LET(la b. W(A(CLS)(b))("p"))(la isp.
  LET(K127)(la K127. LET(K4)(la K4. LET(K3)(la K3. LET(K64)(la K64. LET(K2)(la K2. LET(K0)(la K0. LET(D)(la D.
  LET(FF)(la FF. LET(K1)(la K1. LET(lt)(la T. LET(concat)(la P. LET(add)(la U.
  LET(ord)(la O. LET(str_to_int)(la Y. LET(A)(la A. LET(int_eq)(la Q.

  # RUNP(s)(X)(m) is a loop g; g(g)(i)(c) appends the printable bytes of s
  # from i on to c, stopping at the first other byte or at index m, and then
  # calls X(j)(c') with the index it stopped at. (At i = m the byte read is
  # ignored; str_at past the end is "", whose ord is "0".) RUNP(s) is applied
  # once per write call.
  LET(la s. la X. la m. la g. la i. la c.
      (la h. Q(i)(m)(FF)(isp(Y(O(h))))(la _. g(g)(U(i)(K1))(P(c)(h)))(la _. X(i)(c))(D))(A(s)(i)))(la RUNP.

  # CSIL(k)(s)(n) is a loop g; g(g)(i) skips CSI bytes of s from i on. At the
  # final byte (0x40..0x7E) it calls k(index after it)(0); at the end of s,
  # k(n)(2), the CSI still open.
  LET(la k. la s. la n. la g. la i.
      Q(i)(n)(la _. k(i)(K2))
        (la _. (la b. T(b)(K64)(la _. g(g)(U(i)(K1)))
                        (la _. T(b)(K127)(la _. k(U(i)(K1))(K0))(la _. g(g)(U(i)(K1)))(D))
                        (D))
               (Y(O(A(s)(i)))))
        (D))(la CSIL.

  # ── lists and strings ──
  LET(la n. la c. n(n))(la NIL.
  LET(la h. la t. la n. la c. c(h)(t))(la CONS.
  LET(la s. Y(str_len(s)))(la LEN.
  # SLICE(s)(lo)(hi): bytes lo..hi-1 of s ("" if hi <= lo). Up to 64 bytes
  # are copied by a loop, four a step while it can; longer ranges are halved
  # first, so the cost stays O(n log n).
  LET(la s. la lo. la hi.
      (la sp. sp(sp)(lo)(hi))
      (la sp. la a. la b.
         T(U(a)(K64))(b)
           (la _. (la m. P(sp(sp)(a)(m))(sp(sp)(m)(b)))(div(U(a)(b))(K2)))
           (la _. (la g. g(g)(a)(""))
                  (la g. la i. la c.
                     T(U(i)(K3))(b)
                       (la _. g(g)(U(i)(K4))
                                (P(c)(P(P(A(s)(i))(A(s)(U(i)(K1))))(P(A(s)(U(i)(K2)))(A(s)(U(i)(K3)))))))
                       (la _. T(i)(b)(la _. g(g)(U(i)(K1))(P(c)(A(s)(i))))(la _. c)(D))
                       (D)))
           (D)))(la SLICE.
  LET(la l. (la r. r(r)(l)(NIL))
            (la r. la l. la acc. l(la _. acc)(la h. la t. r(r)(t)(CONS(h)(acc)))))(la REV.
  # JOIN(l): the strings of l concatenated, merged pairwise (balanced)
  LET(la mp. la l. l(la _. NIL)(la a. la t. t(la _. CONS(a)(NIL))(la b. la u. CONS(P(a)(b))(mp(mp)(u)))))(la MP.
  LET(la l. (la j. j(j)(l))
            (la j. la l. l(la _. "")(la a. la t. t(la _. a)(la _. la _. j(j)(MP(MP)(l))))))(la JOIN.
  # TAKE(l)(m): the first m elements of l
  LET(la l. la m. (la tk. tk(tk)(l)(m))
      (la tk. la l. la m. Q(m)(K0)(la _. NIL)
          (la _. l(la _. NIL)(la h. la t. CONS(h)(tk(tk)(t)(sub(m)(K1)))))(D)))(la TAKE.
  # NTH(l)(j)(none)(some): some(element j of l), or none(D) if l is shorter
  LET(la l. la j. la no. la so. (la r. r(r)(l)(j))
      (la r. la l. la j. l(la _. no(D))(la h. la t. Q(j)(K0)(la _. so(h))(la _. r(r)(t)(sub(j)(K1)))(D))))(la NTH.

  # ── the write path (re-bind block, then the helpers it uses) ──
  LET(sub)(la sub. LET(mod)(la mod. LET(K192)(la K192. LET(K91)(la K91.
  LET(K27)(la K27. LET(K10)(la K10. LET(K9)(la K9. LET(K8)(la K8. LET(CAP)(la CAP.
  LET(CAP2)(la CAP2. LET(CSIL)(la CSIL. LET(RUNP)(la RUNP. LET(JOIN)(la JOIN.
  LET(REV)(la REV. LET(SLICE)(la SLICE. LET(TAKE)(la TAKE. LET(CONS)(la CONS.
  LET(NIL)(la NIL. LET(isp)(la isp. LET(FF)(la FF. LET(T)(la T. LET(P)(la P.
  LET(K64)(la K64. LET(K2)(la K2. LET(K0)(la K0. LET(D)(la D. LET(U)(la U. LET(K1)(la K1.
  LET(Y)(la Y. LET(O)(la O. LET(A)(la A. LET(Q)(la Q.
  # ── the output record R = la k. k(L)(N)(S)(C)(K) ──
  LET(la L. la N. la S. la C. la K. la k. k(L)(N)(S)(C)(K))(la MK5.
  LET(MK5(NIL)(K0)(NIL)("")(K0))(la R0.
  LET(la L. la N. la S. la C. la K. C)(la gC.
  LET(la L. la N. la S. la C. la K. K)(la gK.
  # PL(S)(C): the partial line as one string
  LET(la S. la C. S(la _. C)(la _. la _. P(JOIN(REV(S)))(C)))(la PL.
  # PUSH(x)(L)(N)(k): k(L')(N') with x added as the newest line. The list is
  # trimmed to the newest CAP when it reaches CAP2, so the cap costs O(1) a line.
  LET(la x. la L. la N. la k. (la M. Q(M)(CAP2)(la _. k(TAKE(CONS(x)(L))(CAP))(CAP))(la _. k(CONS(x)(L))(M))(D))(U(N)(K1)))(la PUSH.
  # SEG(S)(c)(K)(k): after an append made the length K, move a full tail into
  # the segments: k(S')(C')
  LET(la S. la c. la K. la k. Q(mod(K)(K64))(K0)(la _. k(CONS(c)(S))(""))(la _. k(S)(c))(D))(la SEG.
  # ADV(R)(c)(d): R with the tail replaced by c, which is d bytes longer
  LET(la R. la c. la d. R(la L. la N. la S. la C. la K.
        (la K2. SEG(S)(c)(K2)(la S2. la C2. MK5(L)(N)(S2)(C2)(K2)))(U(K)(d))))(la ADV.
  # DROP(R)(k): R with the partial line's last k bytes dropped (k >= 1; all
  # of it when k >= K). Whole segments are popped and at most one string is
  # sliced (the tail, or the segment the new end falls in), so a run of k
  # '\b' costs one slice, not k.
  LET(la R. la k. R(la L. la N. la S. la C. la K.
      T(k)(K)
        (la _. (la r. T(r)(k)
                  (la _. (la g. g(g)(S)(sub(k)(r)))
                         (la g. la S. la x. S(la _. MK5(L)(N)(NIL)("")(K0))
                            (la h. la t. T(x)(K64)
                               (la _. MK5(L)(N)(t)(SLICE(h)(K0)(sub(K64)(x)))(sub(K)(k)))
                               (la _. Q(x)(K64)(la _. MK5(L)(N)(t)("")(sub(K)(k)))(la _. g(g)(t)(sub(x)(K64)))(D))
                               (D))))
                  (la _. MK5(L)(N)(S)(SLICE(C)(K0)(sub(r)(k)))(sub(K)(k)))
                  (D))
               (mod(K)(K64)))
        (la _. MK5(L)(N)(NIL)("")(K0))
        (D)))(la DROP.
  # SP(m): m spaces, 1 <= m <= 8
  LET(la m. Q(m)(K1)(" ")(Q(m)(K2)("  ")(Q(m)(K3)("   ")(Q(m)(K4)("    ")
            (Q(m)(K5)("     ")(Q(m)(K6)("      ")(Q(m)(K7)("       ")("        "))))))))(la SP.

  # re-bind what the per-byte steps use most
  LET(SP)(la SP. LET(SEG)(la SEG. LET(ADV)(la ADV. LET(PUSH)(la PUSH.
  LET(PL)(la PL. LET(MK5)(la MK5. LET(gC)(la gC. LET(gK)(la gK. LET(NIL)(la NIL.
  LET(K0)(la K0. LET(D)(la D. LET(U)(la U. LET(K1)(la K1. LET(Y)(la Y. LET(O)(la O.
  LET(A)(la A. LET(Q)(la Q.
  # ── write: the control bytes, as pure R -> R' steps ──
  LET(la R. R(la L. la N. la S. la C. la K.
        PUSH(PL(S)(C))(L)(N)(la L2. la N2. MK5(L2)(N2)(NIL)("")(K0))))(la NLR.
  LET(la R. R(la L. la N. la S. la C. la K. (la m. ADV(R)(P(C)(SP(m)))(m))(sub(K8)(mod(K)(K8)))))(la TABR.
  LET(la R. R(la L. la N. la S. la C. la K. ADV(R)(P(C)("?"))(K1)))(la QMR.
  LET(la R. R)(la IDR.
  # re-bind what the dispatch and the loops use
  LET(DROP)(la DROP. LET(P)(la P. LET(sub)(la sub. LET(mod)(la mod. LET(K192)(la K192. LET(K91)(la K91. LET(K9)(la K9.
  LET(K8)(la K8. LET(K2)(la K2. LET(K64)(la K64. LET(K27)(la K27. LET(K10)(la K10.
  LET(CSIL)(la CSIL. LET(RUNP)(la RUNP. LET(PUSH)(la PUSH. LET(PL)(la PL. LET(MK5)(la MK5.
  LET(ADV)(la ADV. LET(NLR)(la NLR. LET(gC)(la gC. LET(gK)(la gK. LET(isp)(la isp.
  LET(NIL)(la NIL. LET(T)(la T. LET(FF)(la FF. LET(K0)(la K0. LET(D)(la D. LET(U)(la U.
  LET(K1)(la K1. LET(Y)(la Y. LET(O)(la O. LET(A)(la A. LET(Q)(la Q.
  # CSEL(b): the step for control byte b (not printable, not ESC, not '\b')
  LET(la b. Q(b)(K10)(NLR)(Q(b)(K9)(TABR)(T(b)(K192)(IDR)(QMR))))(la CSEL.

  # ── write: the loops ──
  # A step has the shape h(o)(i)(R): o is the outer loop, i the index of the
  # byte that chose the step, R the output record. The per-call values (the
  # bytes s, their length n, the exit ret) are applied to the steps ONCE per
  # write call, so a step's CLOSE rescans cover only its last three
  # parameters' small body.
  #
  # RBS(s)(rp)(o)(i)(j)(R): a run of printable bytes from i stopped at a '\b'
  # at j (j > i). The '\b' drops the run's own last byte, so the run is
  # taken without it, and the outer loop goes on after the '\b': a one-byte
  # run leaves R as it was (x\b, spinners), a two-byte run appends its first
  # byte (overstrike, _\bA_\bB: the run A_ gives A), a longer one is redone
  # to j-1. Nothing is sliced.
  LET(la s. la rp. la o. la i. la j. la R.
      (la L. Q(L)(K1)(la _. o(o)(U(j)(K1))(R))
        (la _. Q(L)(K2)(la _. o(o)(U(j)(K1))(ADV(R)(P(R(gC))(A(s)(i)))(K1)))
          (la _. (la g. g(g)(i)(R(gC)))(rp(la e. la c. o(o)(U(j)(K1))(ADV(R)(c)(sub(e)(i))))(sub(j)(K1))))
          (D))
        (D))(sub(j)(i)))(la RBS.
  # RUNS(s)(rp)(n): the step for a printable byte (rp is RUNP(s)): run RUNP
  # over the bytes, at most to the end of the current 64-byte segment. A run
  # that stops at a '\n' ends the line at once (the common case), one that
  # stops at a '\b' goes to RBS; anything else goes back to the outer loop.
  LET(la s. la rp. la n. la o. la i. la R.
      (la g. g(g)(i)(R(gC)))
      (rp(la j. la c. (la b. Q(b)(K10)
                        (la _. R(la L. la N. la S. la C. la K.
                                 PUSH(PL(S)(c))(L)(N)(la L2. la N2. o(o)(U(j)(K1))(MK5(L2)(N2)(NIL)("")(K0)))))
                        (la _. Q(b)(K8)(la _. RBS(s)(rp)(o)(i)(j)(R))(la _. o(o)(j)(ADV(R)(c)(sub(j)(i))))(D))
                        (D))(Y(O(A(s)(j)))))
         ((la m. T(m)(n)(m)(n))(U(i)(sub(K64)(mod(R(gK))(K64)))))))(la RUNS.
  # CSIS(s)(n)(ret)(o)(i)(R): skip a CSI whose parameter bytes start at i
  LET(la s. la n. la ret. la o. la i. la R.
      (la g. g(g)(i))(CSIL(la j. la e. Q(e)(K0)(la _. o(o)(j)(R))(la _. ret(R)(K2))(D))(s)(n)))(la CSIS.
  # ESC1(s)(n)(ret)(cs)(o)(i)(R): the step for an ESC at index i (cs is
